's $33M-to-$11M fundraising edge in a Democratic-favorable midterm environment", "Sullivan's incumbency and Alaska's ranked-choice runoff keep the margin tight"], "rationale": "October surveys show Peltola narrowly ahead, including a Republican-sponsored poll with her up two, and Kalshi prices her near 75 percent; raters split between tossup and lean Democratic with none favoring Sullivan. His incumbency and the state's Republican lean keep this close, but the polling trend, debate reception, and money edge justify a modest Democratic lean.", "watch": "A late-breaking negative story about Peltola or Sullivan consolidating Republican voters as ranked-choice tabulation proceeds could flip the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Dan Sullivan (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Alaska's Senate race pairs incumbent Republican Dan Sullivan with Democrat Mary Peltola, and it sits at the center of the battle for Senate control. October surveys show Peltola narrowly ahead: a New York Times/Siena poll had her up seven in early October, and later polls from both Democratic- and Republican-sponsored firms show a one-to-two-point race in her direction. Eleven race raters split between tossup and lean Democratic, and prediction markets give Peltola roughly a three-in-four chance.\n\nPeltola enters with large financial advantages, having raised about $33 million to Sullivan's $11 million, plus strong reviews from the mid-October debates. Sullivan counters with incumbency, name recognition, and a state that leans Republican by several points. Ranked-choice tabulation and late-breaking stories about both candidates add uncertainty, but the evidence tilts modestly toward Peltola.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5747, "margin": 0.87}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6955, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6995, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6965, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6035, "margin": 2.0}, 